# `next` is what the open has read off the iterator. Where it is the engine's own `next` of generators, the call would resume the body and make an object of what the body hands out, which the loop alone would read.
# So the step asks the function that was read whether it is that one, and resumes the body itself: no call, no object, no read of `done` and `value`. Nothing is asked of the place that `next` was read from.
# The call stays for every other `next`, for an iterator that is no generator, and for a generator that is running or has ended, which the method answers.
# The arm costs its bytes at every loop, so it stands where a generator is what is proven or expected of what the loop iterates over, and at a `yield*`.
# a plain `for of` over what is expected to be a generator asks once, at its open: generator-is-asked-once-where-the-loop-opens.ir. So the step of `each` reads what is returned, as a step of `yield*` does, and goes by the protocol
# function eachOfList(things: number[]) { for (const thing of things) keep(thing); }
# function eachOfAnything(things) { for (const thing of things) keep(thing); }
